                "math-negative-numbers",
                "Number foundations",
                10,
                "Negative numbers: below zero",
                "Numbers that point the other way from a starting line.",
                "A negative number is a distance in the opposite direction from zero, so what it means depends on where zero is.",
                "## The idea in plain words\nEvery negative number is measured from a zero, and that zero is a starting line someone picked. Positive numbers go one way from it; negative numbers go the other way. The minus sign does not mean \"bad\" or \"smaller in importance.\" It means \"the other direction.\"\n\n## Where you'll see it\n- **Sea level.** 0 ft is the surface of the ocean. A plane at 30,000 ft is above it; a submarine at −400 ft is 400 feet below it.\n- **Temperature.** On a winter morning in Tulsa it might be −6°F: six degrees below the zero mark on the thermometer.\n- **Money.** Spend $30 more than you have and your balance is −$30: you owe $30.\n- **Games.** On a game map, walking left from the center makes your x position go down, past 0 and into negative numbers. A resource readout of −5 per second means you are spending faster than you earn.\n\n## What the symbols stand for\nThere are no letters yet, only signed numbers. Read −400 as \"400 in the negative direction.\" The size without the direction is called the **absolute value**, written with bars: |−400| = 400, meaning \"400 away from zero.\"\n\n## The rules, explained\n1. **Adding a negative moves you backward.** It's 5°F and the temperature changes by −12°F. Start at 5 and step down 12: five steps get you to 0, seven more land you at −7°F.\n2. **Subtracting a negative moves you forward.** Removing a debt makes you richer. If you owe $20 (−20) and the debt is cancelled, −20 − (−20) = 0.\n3. **The change between two positions is \"end minus start.\"** A submarine dives from −100 ft to −340 ft: −340 − (−100) = −240. The minus sign tells you it went down; 240 tells you how far.\n4. **Multiplying by a positive number scales the distance and keeps the direction.** A submarine four times as deep as one at −60 ft sits at 4 × (−60) = −240 ft: still below the surface, just farther down.\n5. **Multiplying two negatives gives a positive.** Think of it as reversing a reversal. A tank drains 3 gallons per hour (−3). Two hours *ago* (−2) it held more water, not less: (−3) × (−2) = +6 gallons more.\n\n## Watch out\n\"Four times as cold\" sounds natural, but it doesn't work with temperature. 0°F isn't \"no heat,\" so there is no sensible answer to \"what is four times as cold as 0°F?\" Use multiplication only when zero really means \"none of it,\" like depth below the surface or money owed. Temperature is perfect for adding and subtracting.",
                "Depth below sea level (0 ft = the surface)\nEnemy vessel:  −60 ft\nYour sub:      4 × (−60) = −240 ft    (four times as deep)\nGap:           −240 − (−60) = −180 ft (you are 180 ft deeper)",
                PrecalculusNumeric("Compare two depths", "Your submarine is at −350 ft. The enemy vessel is at −90 ft. How many feet deeper is your submarine?", 260.0d, 0.5d, "ft", "Find the gap with end minus start: −350 − (−90). Subtracting a negative turns into adding.", "We want the distance between the two positions, so subtract one from the other.\n−350 − (−90): subtracting a negative is the same as adding, so this becomes −350 + 90.\nStarting at −350 and moving 90 toward the surface lands at −260.\nThe minus sign only says \"downward.\" The distance is 260, so your sub is 260 ft deeper.", PrecalculusUnits(("ft", 1.0d), ("m", 3.28084d))),
                null,
