            new(
                "math-linear-equations",
                "Equation foundations",
                60,
                "Solving linear equations",
                "Undo what was done to the unknown, one step at a time.",
                "Solving an equation means undoing the operations around the unknown in reverse order, doing the same thing to both sides so they stay balanced.",
                "## The idea in plain words\nAn equation says two things are equal, like a balanced scale. To find the unknown, you peel away everything that was done to it. Whatever you do to one side, you must do to the other, or the scale tips and the statement stops being true.\n\nYou undo steps in **reverse order**, the same way you take off shoes before socks even though you put socks on first.\n\n## Where you'll see it\n- **Saving up:** \"I have $200 and save $15 a week. When will I have $950?\"\n- **Fuel:** \"How far can I drive before the tank hits the reserve line?\"\n- **Games:** \"I have 200 gold and earn 15 per second. When can I afford the 950-gold unit?\"\n\n## What the letters stand for\nIn 200 + 15t = 950:\n- **t** is the unknown time in seconds, the **variable** you're solving for.\n- **200** is the starting bank, a **constant**.\n- **15** is the income per second, a **constant** (the coefficient of t).\n- **950** is the goal, a **constant**.\n\n## Walk through one problem\nSolve 200 + 15t = 950.\n1. **What was done to t?** First it was multiplied by 15, then 200 was added. Undo in reverse: remove the 200 first.\n2. **Subtract 200 from both sides:** 15t = 750. In words: after the starting bank, you still need 750 more gold.\n3. **Divide both sides by 15:** t = 50. In words: earning 15 per second, 750 takes 50 seconds.\n4. **Check by plugging back in:** 200 + 15 × 50 = 200 + 750 = 950. ✓\n\n## Watch out\nAlways check your answer by substituting it back into the original equation. It takes ten seconds and catches most mistakes.",
                "200 + 15t = 950        start + income × time = goal\n      15t = 750        subtract 200 from both sides\n        t = 50         divide both sides by 15\ncheck: 200 + 15 × 50 = 950 ✓",
                PrecalculusNumeric("Drive to the reserve line", "A delivery van has 12 gallons in its tank and burns 0.4 gallons per mile. The driver must stop before the tank drops below 2 gallons: 12 − 0.4m = 2. How many miles can the van drive?", 25.0d, 0.05d, "mi", "Undo the 12 first, then undo the multiplication by 0.4. Watch the signs.", "The equation 12 − 0.4m = 2 says: start with 12 gallons, burn 0.4 per mile, end at 2.\nSubtract 12 from both sides: −0.4m = −10. In words: the van can burn 10 gallons.\nDivide both sides by −0.4: m = 25. In words: 10 gallons at 0.4 per mile is 25 miles.\nCheck: 12 − 0.4 × 25 = 12 − 10 = 2 ✓. The van can drive 25 miles.", PrecalculusUnits(("mi", 1.0d), ("km", 0.621371d))),
                null,
