            new(
                "math-coordinate-plane",
                "Function foundations",
                80,
                "The coordinate plane and distance",
                "Two numbers pin down any spot on a flat map.",
                "An (x, y) pair locates a point on a grid, and the straight-line distance between two points comes from the right triangle their gaps make.",
                "## The idea in plain words\nA coordinate plane is a map grid. The **x** number says how far left or right you are; the **y** number says how far up or down. The point (0, 0), where both lines cross, is the **origin**: the spot everything else is measured from.\n\n## Where you'll see it\n- **Game maps.** Every unit on a strategy map has an (x, y) position. A minimap is just this grid shrunk down.\n- **Screens.** A pixel at (300, 120) is 300 pixels from the left edge. Heads up: on screens and in many game engines, y grows *downward*, the opposite of math class.\n- **Real maps.** Longitude and latitude work like x and y for the Earth (over short distances).\n\n## What the letters stand for\nFor two points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂), the small numbers are labels meaning \"first point\" and \"second point,\" not exponents.\n- **x₂ − x₁** is the horizontal gap: how far apart they are left-to-right.\n- **y₂ − y₁** is the vertical gap.\n- **d** is the straight-line distance, what a unit flying directly would travel.\n\n## Finding the distance, explained\nThe horizontal gap and the vertical gap meet at a square corner, forming a right triangle. The straight line between the points is the triangle's long side. The **Pythagorean theorem** says: the long side squared equals the two short sides squared, added together.\n\nIn words: square the horizontal gap, square the vertical gap, add them, then take the square root to undo the squaring.\n\n## Walk through one problem\nYour scout is at (1, 2) and the enemy base is at (7, 10).\n1. Horizontal gap: 7 − 1 = 6.\n2. Vertical gap: 10 − 2 = 8.\n3. Square and add: 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100.\n4. Square root: √100 = 10. The base is 10 grid units away.\n\n## Watch out\nGaps can come out negative if you subtract in the other order. That's fine: squaring a negative makes it positive, so the distance is the same either way.",
                "scout (1, 2) → base (7, 10)\nhorizontal gap = 7 − 1  = 6\nvertical gap   = 10 − 2 = 8\ndistance = √(6² + 8²) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10",
                PrecalculusNumeric("Check artillery range", "Your artillery sits at (4, 3) on the map grid and the enemy radar is at (16, 8). Each grid square is 1 km. How far apart are they?", 13.0d, 0.05d, "km", "Find the horizontal and vertical gaps, square each, add them, then take the square root.", "Horizontal gap: 16 − 4 = 12 km.\nVertical gap: 8 − 3 = 5 km.\nThese two gaps are the short sides of a right triangle. Square and add: 12² + 5² = 144 + 25 = 169.\nUndo the squaring with a square root: √169 = 13. They're 13 km apart.", PrecalculusUnits(("km", 1.0d), ("m", 0.001d))),
                null,
